WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.640
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there is this expectation of extreme

00:07.640 --> 00:14.520
precision. Like, you break your arm, right? Right, you go get an x-ray. Exactly. The x-ray shows that

00:14.520 --> 00:19.260
jagged white line and the doctor just points at the screen and says, well, there it is. It is

00:19.260 --> 00:24.960
entirely binary. It is either broken or not broken. You can literally see the exact point of failure.

00:24.960 --> 00:31.620
Yeah, and it's clean. But then you step into the world of complex systemic issues,

00:32.160 --> 00:38.680
say neurodevelopment or certain types of trauma. And suddenly that x-ray machine is completely

00:38.680 --> 00:45.400
useless. Right. You're looking at a diagnostic landscape that is just entirely murky. You have

00:45.400 --> 00:51.460
a tool that is fundamentally the wrong instrument for the job, but, you know, it is the only tool

00:51.460 --> 00:56.080
you have ever been taught to use. You're basically trying to measure water volume with a ruler.

00:56.640 --> 01:02.060
The tool simply does not map to the reality of the condition you are observing. Okay, so let's

01:02.060 --> 01:07.940
unpack this. Today, we are taking that exact same murky diagnostic lens and we are pointing it

01:07.940 --> 01:14.980
straight at something you probably look at every single day. Your phone. Yes, we are going to

01:14.980 --> 01:20.540
completely dismantle and radically redefine what social media actually is. We are moving far,

01:20.540 --> 01:25.520
far away from the toxic, doom-scrolling feeds that you are probably picturing right now in your head.

01:25.520 --> 01:30.920
And instead, we are going to discover the hidden architecture of true online communities.

01:31.460 --> 01:35.480
It requires a fundamental shift in how we perceive digital interaction.

01:36.100 --> 01:41.760
To do this, we are using a really groundbreaking October 2026 paper by the independent researcher

01:41.760 --> 01:49.760
Flyxion. It is titled, The Sociality of Computation, From Educational Systems to Persistent Digital Worlds.

01:49.760 --> 01:55.480
And what Flyxion does so effectively here is argue that our current universally accepted

01:55.480 --> 02:00.340
definition of social media is, in fact, a massive misdiagnosis.

02:00.560 --> 02:01.400
A misdiagnosis.

02:01.700 --> 02:04.540
Yes, we have been looking at the wrong symptoms for decades.

02:04.860 --> 02:10.680
So our mission today for this deep dive is to show you that you aren't actually trapped in the

02:10.680 --> 02:12.800
age of algorithmically ranked feeds.

02:13.020 --> 02:13.260
Right.

02:13.260 --> 02:17.840
You might be listening to this right now thinking, well, I kind of like my TikTok algorithm.

02:18.380 --> 02:20.080
It knows exactly what I want to see.

02:20.540 --> 02:23.220
And that is perfectly fine for passive entertainment.

02:23.360 --> 02:24.120
Sure. Absolutely.

02:24.660 --> 02:30.120
But if you are looking for connection for real community, that endless stream of broadcasted

02:30.120 --> 02:31.460
takes is a dead end.

02:31.460 --> 02:37.980
By understanding what Flyxion calls shared computational objects, you are going to get a literal map.

02:38.300 --> 02:42.880
A map to finding and maybe even building better, more enduring spaces online.

02:43.260 --> 02:46.040
The core thesis is highly optimistic, actually.

02:46.280 --> 02:46.700
It is.

02:47.120 --> 02:52.760
Flyxion argues that sociality, the actual forming of durable communities, that feeling of belonging

02:52.760 --> 02:57.660
to a collective, it does not emerge from user profiles and ranked feeds.

02:57.800 --> 03:00.080
Wait, so if it doesn't come from feeds, where does it come from?

03:00.080 --> 03:03.120
It emerges from shared history and accessible objects.

03:03.460 --> 03:08.760
It is about what we build, modify, and maintain together, rather than just what we broadcast

03:08.760 --> 03:09.500
at each other.

03:09.720 --> 03:10.240
I love that.

03:10.680 --> 03:15.140
But to get there, we have to start by tearing down your preconceived notions of what social

03:15.140 --> 03:16.220
media even is.

03:16.300 --> 03:17.300
We have to clear the slate.

03:17.480 --> 03:17.740
Right.

03:18.120 --> 03:21.560
When I say social media, what is the architecture that immediately pops into your head?

03:22.000 --> 03:24.140
For almost everyone listening, it is identical.

03:24.740 --> 03:28.100
It is Facebook, X, Instagram, TikTok.

03:28.100 --> 03:32.700
And that dominant architecture relies on very specific, rigid components.

03:33.260 --> 03:34.600
First, you have a personal profile.

03:34.920 --> 03:37.000
Which acts as your digital identity card.

03:37.140 --> 03:37.460
Exactly.

03:37.820 --> 03:41.860
Then you have a formalized list of connections, your friends, your followers, subscribers.

03:42.460 --> 03:44.160
And crucially, you have the feed.

03:44.560 --> 03:44.840
Right.

03:45.060 --> 03:46.060
A ranked stream of posts.

03:46.440 --> 03:46.680
Right.

03:46.680 --> 03:50.180
And it is not just casual users who think this way.

03:50.320 --> 03:55.020
There is a massive academic precedent that locked this exact definition into place.

03:55.420 --> 03:55.460
Yeah.

03:55.620 --> 04:01.920
Flyxion points directly to Boyd and Ellison's famous 2007 definition of social network sites.

04:02.580 --> 04:06.040
Boyd and Ellison essentially codified the era of Web 2.0.

04:06.040 --> 04:12.680
They stated that a social network is a web-based service where individuals construct a public

04:12.680 --> 04:14.060
or semi-public profile.

04:14.700 --> 04:17.660
Articulate a list of other users with whom they share a connection.

04:18.260 --> 04:22.360
And view their list of connections and those made by others within the system.

04:22.820 --> 04:24.540
That was the gold standard definition.

04:25.100 --> 04:29.020
Which, to be fair, was an incredibly useful definition back in 2007.

04:29.520 --> 04:33.980
It helped researchers wrap their heads around what MySpace and early Facebook were doing.

04:33.980 --> 04:37.880
It described the phenomenon of that specific moment perfectly.

04:38.560 --> 04:44.960
But Fleikerson argues that carrying this definition forward into the modern era is a massive category error.

04:45.500 --> 04:46.680
Okay, a category error.

04:46.900 --> 04:47.780
Break that down for us.

04:48.060 --> 04:50.560
A category error is a philosophical concept.

04:50.900 --> 04:55.740
It means you fundamentally misunderstand the ontological nature of the thing you are observing.

04:56.100 --> 04:58.940
So we are just categorizing the entire internet wrong.

04:59.340 --> 05:00.220
Essentially, yes.

05:00.220 --> 05:06.280
By defining online sociality strictly through the lens of profiles, follower graphs, and feeds,

05:06.860 --> 05:12.340
we are mistaking one specific historical branch of social computing for the trunk of the entire tree.

05:12.520 --> 05:12.880
Oh, I see.

05:13.540 --> 05:16.960
We are looking at a single offshoot of digital evolution and declaring,

05:17.120 --> 05:19.080
hey, this is the entire species.

05:19.540 --> 05:20.040
Precisely.

05:20.200 --> 05:20.660
Precisely.

05:21.120 --> 05:26.200
And by making that error, we end up completely ignoring massive, genuinely social systems

05:26.200 --> 05:28.360
that millions of people use every single day.

05:28.360 --> 05:30.140
Right. Think about Wikipedia.

05:30.580 --> 05:34.240
Or think about GitHub, where developers literally build the world's software.

05:34.720 --> 05:35.580
Or YouTube.

05:36.140 --> 05:39.880
Or massive multiplayer digital worlds like Minecraft and EVE Online.

05:40.140 --> 05:42.580
What do all of those platforms actually have in common?

05:43.300 --> 05:49.080
None of them rely on prominent personal profiles or personal broadcast-style feeds as their main thing.

05:49.380 --> 05:54.020
Yet they account for a staggering fraction of the sustained collaborative activity on the internet.

05:54.640 --> 05:56.400
It is wild to really think about it.

05:56.400 --> 05:58.620
Take Wikipedia as an example.

05:59.320 --> 06:05.840
It sustains deeply complex cooperation among thousands of people who, frankly, frequently do not know each other at all.

06:06.140 --> 06:08.160
Right. They aren't following each other's personal lives.

06:08.220 --> 06:10.260
They don't care what the other editor had for breakfast.

06:10.500 --> 06:10.720
No.

06:11.120 --> 06:14.760
They communicate almost entirely through the object they are jointly producing,

06:14.900 --> 06:17.080
which is the encyclopedic article itself.

06:17.080 --> 06:23.840
So, thinking social media is only feeds is like saying a city is only made of highways,

06:24.440 --> 06:30.880
while completely ignoring the public squares, the libraries, the construction sites where people actually gather to do things.

06:31.100 --> 06:33.200
That is a highly accurate way to visualize it.

06:33.560 --> 06:37.980
The highways, the feeds, they just move traffic from point A to point B at high speed.

06:38.380 --> 06:40.760
But the squares are where the city actually happens.

06:40.760 --> 06:41.480
Exactly.

06:42.100 --> 06:47.600
Flyxion traces the origin of this highway mentality back to early telecommunications.

06:47.940 --> 06:54.240
The dominant model of online social life descends directly from the telephone exchange and the postal service.

06:54.460 --> 06:57.500
It operates on what we'd call a communication channel model.

06:57.820 --> 06:58.060
Right.

06:58.380 --> 07:03.500
I write a letter, I put your address on it, the post office routes it, and you receive it.

07:03.660 --> 07:07.000
The medium is just a passive carrier of the message.

07:07.000 --> 07:13.160
And even when you post publicly on a modern feed, say, you know, you send out a tweet or a status update,

07:13.580 --> 07:18.120
you are essentially just sending a broadcast message to an aggregated audience.

07:18.760 --> 07:24.140
It is still fundamentally a system of people throwing discrete messages at other people.

07:24.720 --> 07:29.640
So, if the feed is just a giant digital post office, what is Flyxion's alternative?

07:29.820 --> 07:31.880
How do we redefine the city square?

07:32.440 --> 07:35.280
The alternative is an object-centered model.

07:35.580 --> 07:36.700
Okay, object-centered.

07:36.700 --> 07:41.160
Instead of communication being mediated by messages traveling between persons,

07:41.880 --> 07:46.300
the sociality is mediated by persistent digital objects.

07:46.880 --> 07:49.200
So, let's go back to editing a Wikipedia article.

07:49.440 --> 07:53.460
When you make a contribution there, you aren't addressing the next reader directly.

07:54.100 --> 07:56.340
You might not know or care who the next reader is.

07:56.480 --> 07:58.100
You are addressing the article.

07:58.560 --> 08:04.520
Your relationship with the next person to come along is mediated entirely by the altered state of that shared thing.

08:04.700 --> 08:05.100
Exactly.

08:05.100 --> 08:06.920
It is not me talking to you.

08:07.060 --> 08:10.720
It is me walking into a room, painting a wall blue, and leaving.

08:11.380 --> 08:13.660
Then you walk into the room and experience a blue wall.

08:14.140 --> 08:14.440
Yes.

08:15.140 --> 08:16.980
And to ground this mechanically,

08:17.240 --> 08:21.300
Flyxion pulls in a foundational concept from the study of cooperative work,

08:21.560 --> 08:22.940
dating back to 1989.

08:22.940 --> 08:26.820
Star and Grissimer's concept of boundary objects.

08:26.820 --> 08:33.980
I want to spend some real time on this because understanding the boundary object is basically the key to this entire deep dive.

08:34.540 --> 08:36.780
What exactly did Star and Grissimer study?

08:36.780 --> 08:45.480
They conducted a sociological study of a natural history museum, specifically the Museum of Vertebrate Zoology at UC Berkeley in its early years.

08:45.480 --> 08:51.940
They looked at how wildly different groups of people managed to cooperate to build this museum,

08:52.100 --> 08:56.640
even though they fundamentally disagreed on what the museum's actual purpose was.

08:56.880 --> 08:59.380
Because you had a crazy mix of people involved in this, right?

08:59.380 --> 09:03.980
You had amateur bird watchers out in the field, professional academic biologists.

09:04.400 --> 09:05.600
University administrators.

09:06.060 --> 09:07.560
And professional trappers and hunters.

09:07.800 --> 09:08.160
Exactly.

09:08.640 --> 09:13.160
The administrator's primary goal is just to balance a budget and keep the university happy.

09:13.160 --> 09:17.120
The biologist wants to publish a groundbreaking taxonomy paper.

09:17.700 --> 09:20.880
The amateur bird watcher is doing it for the love of nature and conservation.

09:21.620 --> 09:25.040
And the trapper simply wants to get paid a bounty for a physical specimen.

09:25.680 --> 09:30.160
They have completely divergent, sometimes totally conflicting worldviews.

09:30.260 --> 09:34.400
If they sat in a room and tried to talk to each other, they would probably argue constantly.

09:34.980 --> 09:37.580
But they didn't have to talk to each other directly to cooperate.

09:37.660 --> 09:37.800
No.

09:38.560 --> 09:42.880
They collaborated successfully through what Star and Grissimer called boundary objects.

09:43.160 --> 09:48.040
The physical specimens themselves, the standardized field notes, the maps of the territory.

09:48.880 --> 09:50.120
Okay, break that down for me.

09:50.760 --> 09:53.240
How does a dead bird act as a boundary object?

09:53.940 --> 09:57.820
A boundary object is something that is plastic enough to adapt to the local,

09:58.020 --> 09:59.720
specific needs of different communities,

09:59.920 --> 10:03.480
but robust enough to maintain a common identity across all of them.

10:03.900 --> 10:04.460
That makes sense.

10:04.560 --> 10:07.020
To the trapper, the dead bird is a commodity.

10:07.360 --> 10:08.280
It represents a paycheck.

10:08.920 --> 10:13.140
To the administrator, that exact same bird is a line item on an expense report.

10:13.160 --> 10:19.080
And to the biologist, it is a crucial data point proving a species mutation.

10:19.880 --> 10:24.380
So the shared object makes coordination possible among parties whose purposes differ,

10:24.700 --> 10:30.720
because the object can be interpreted in multiple ways while remaining physically the same thing.

10:31.100 --> 10:31.360
Yes.

10:31.540 --> 10:32.740
It acts as an anchor.

10:33.160 --> 10:36.660
It anchors all these disparate motivations into a single shared reality.

10:36.660 --> 10:43.060
And mapping this to the digital world, the boundary object isn't a stuffed bird in a drawer.

10:43.280 --> 10:46.180
It is a digital flashcard on a learning platform.

10:46.380 --> 10:48.560
It is a repository of code on GitHub.

10:49.120 --> 10:52.360
Or a digital castle built on a Minecraft server.

10:52.780 --> 10:53.480
Exactly right.

10:54.140 --> 10:57.700
But wait, if any digital thing we interact with is a boundary object,

10:57.700 --> 11:02.900
doesn't the definition of social media become so broad that it is basically meaningless?

11:03.420 --> 11:06.420
Like, is a parking meter app social media?

11:06.900 --> 11:09.140
Is my digital thermostat social media?

11:09.560 --> 11:11.360
That is a very crucial pushback.

11:11.740 --> 11:13.440
And Flyxion anticipates this.

11:13.980 --> 11:18.900
The paper sets up a rigorous stress test to pinpoint exactly when computational infrastructure

11:18.900 --> 11:22.880
crosses the threshold into becoming a genuinely social medium.

11:23.240 --> 11:24.780
Okay, walk us through these scenarios.

11:24.780 --> 11:25.980
Scenario one.

11:26.620 --> 11:29.340
Imagine two people sitting in the exact same physical room.

11:29.980 --> 11:34.280
Each person is holding a separate offline calculator doing their own math homework.

11:34.800 --> 11:37.200
Is that a social computational system?

11:37.640 --> 11:38.360
Obviously not.

11:38.800 --> 11:40.440
I mean, they are co-located physically.

11:40.940 --> 11:43.600
But digitally, they are in isolated universes.

11:44.040 --> 11:44.340
Correct.

11:44.860 --> 11:45.600
Scenario two.

11:46.240 --> 11:49.680
Someone fills out a complex web form, maybe a tax return, and hits submit.

11:49.980 --> 11:54.500
The data is saved to a secure, private, encrypted database sitting on a server somewhere.

11:54.780 --> 11:56.260
No one else has access to it.

11:56.700 --> 11:57.440
Still not social.

11:57.760 --> 11:58.760
There is no access.

11:59.000 --> 12:00.700
There is no shared visibility at all.

12:00.940 --> 12:01.200
Right.

12:01.800 --> 12:02.720
Now, scenario three.

12:03.280 --> 12:08.700
I create a highly detailed open access spreadsheet on a cloud server and publish the link publicly.

12:08.700 --> 12:10.620
Okay, this is where it gets nuanced.

12:11.480 --> 12:15.360
You have created the structural capacity for sociality.

12:15.940 --> 12:16.440
It is public.

12:16.900 --> 12:17.600
It is accessible.

12:18.920 --> 12:27.560
But if I create that spreadsheet and I tweet the link and literally zero people ever click it, is it social media?

12:28.560 --> 12:30.240
I would argue no.

12:30.860 --> 12:33.080
It is just a lonely file sitting on a server.

12:33.080 --> 12:35.420
And Flyxion would entirely agree with you.

12:35.620 --> 12:37.860
It is only structurally available.

12:38.280 --> 12:47.320
But the moment you open that link and you see that I am currently typing in cell A1 and I change a formula that alters the graph you are currently looking at on your screen.

12:47.560 --> 12:47.840
Boom.

12:48.340 --> 12:51.300
Now we have achieved realized social generativity.

12:51.680 --> 12:52.040
Exactly.

12:52.600 --> 12:56.700
Because your reality was just altered by my action on the shared object.

12:56.960 --> 12:59.880
The shared object is the locus of the interaction.

13:00.380 --> 13:02.260
It absolutely requires three things.

13:02.260 --> 13:09.660
Accessibility, observable changes, and what the paper terms, cross-participant consequence.

13:10.220 --> 13:11.760
Cross-participant consequence.

13:12.360 --> 13:18.980
Meaning my action must fundamentally alter what you can perceive, what you can interpret, or what you can do next.

13:19.260 --> 13:19.600
Yes.

13:20.240 --> 13:24.820
And this realization completely shatters the timeline of internet history we have all been taught.

13:24.980 --> 13:25.220
Right.

13:25.220 --> 13:31.060
We have this mythology that social media was invented by Mark Zuckerberg in a Harvard dorm room in 2004.

13:31.560 --> 13:34.620
Or maybe Tom Anderson with MySpace in 2003.

13:35.020 --> 13:43.060
But if we define social media as cross-participant consequence on shared objects, we have to travel way back to the 1960s.

13:43.060 --> 13:47.340
And we have to look at a field that nobody associates with the birth of digital communities.

13:47.820 --> 13:48.300
Education.

13:49.020 --> 13:49.420
Education.

13:50.240 --> 13:50.940
Not defense.

13:51.420 --> 13:52.840
Not communication software.

13:53.480 --> 13:55.420
But giant mechanical textbooks.

13:56.060 --> 13:58.880
This is one of the most fascinating historical corrections in the paper.

13:59.160 --> 14:03.060
The true birthplace of digital sociality was educational infrastructure.

14:03.680 --> 14:05.100
Specifically a system called Plato.

14:05.100 --> 14:07.440
Okay, let's set the scene for the listener here.

14:07.560 --> 14:12.860
Because when we say computer in 1960, we are talking about a sleek laptop.

14:13.180 --> 14:13.620
Not at all.

14:14.340 --> 14:18.540
Plato stands for Programmed Logic for Automatic Teaching Operations.

14:18.960 --> 14:21.980
It was developed at the University of Illinois starting in 1960.

14:22.440 --> 14:27.120
We are talking about massive, room-sized, mainframe computers.

14:27.480 --> 14:31.420
And the designers of Plato had zero intention of building a social network, right?

14:31.420 --> 14:31.640
Zero.

14:31.640 --> 14:37.400
The system was built strictly and exclusively to deliver automated instruction to students.

14:38.080 --> 14:42.440
To facilitate this, the engineers built a specific programming language called Tudor.

14:42.720 --> 14:43.360
Tudor, okay.

14:43.700 --> 14:52.480
Tudor was designed to be highly accessible so that professors and instructors who were not computer scientists could write interactive lesson materials and quizzes.

14:53.060 --> 14:56.820
And by the early 1970s, they had advanced the hardware significantly.

14:57.000 --> 15:01.460
They were using these iconic glowing orange plasma display terminals.

15:01.640 --> 15:02.820
Yes, the orange screens.

15:03.360 --> 15:08.800
They could mix text and vector graphics on the same screen, which was totally revolutionary at the time.

15:08.980 --> 15:16.180
But the critical architectural feature of Plato was that all these terminals were connected to a central mainframe with shared memory.

15:16.440 --> 15:17.420
Ah, shared memory.

15:17.420 --> 15:26.260
And because the Tudor language was so accessible, the users, the students and the amateurs using the terminals, did what humans always do.

15:26.680 --> 15:29.060
They engaged in creative appropriation.

15:29.560 --> 15:33.140
They accidentally birthed the precursor to modern online communities.

15:33.780 --> 15:36.420
They were supposed to be doing automated calculus drills.

15:36.760 --> 15:36.960
Right.

15:36.960 --> 15:40.640
But instead, they realized, wait, this machine remembers what I type.

15:41.000 --> 15:43.320
And someone on another terminal can see it.

15:44.040 --> 15:45.900
So they started building worlds.

15:46.820 --> 15:50.540
They built some of the first multiplayer digital games in human history.

15:51.220 --> 15:56.220
Games like Empire, which was an incredibly complex multiplayer space strategy game.

15:56.220 --> 15:59.500
Or Avatar, an early graphical role-playing game.

15:59.860 --> 16:02.440
Or Air Fight, a 3D flight simulator.

16:03.220 --> 16:05.980
And these weren't just cute little weekend side projects.

16:06.180 --> 16:07.000
Far from it.

16:07.940 --> 16:15.900
Encyclopedic accounts of the Plato system report that these specific games logged more than a million contact hours each by the users.

16:16.160 --> 16:17.420
Let that sink in.

16:17.420 --> 16:25.880
A million hours of user engagement in the 1970s, long before the personal computer revolution even happened.

16:26.660 --> 16:29.060
And the innovation extended far beyond gaming, too.

16:29.780 --> 16:34.100
In 1973, a user named Doug Brown wrote a program called Talkomatic.

16:34.460 --> 16:37.540
It was essentially the world's first real-time chat room.

16:37.900 --> 16:40.400
You could have up to five people typing simultaneously, right?

16:40.580 --> 16:40.880
Yes.

16:41.060 --> 16:46.000
And each person's text appeared in a separate horizontal window on that orange plasma screen.

16:46.000 --> 16:54.280
But the holy grail of this era, and the absolute perfect example of Flyxion's object-centered sociality, was a program called Plato Notes.

16:54.660 --> 16:56.280
Created by David R. Woolley.

16:57.360 --> 17:00.540
What was the original intended purpose of Plato Notes?

17:01.040 --> 17:07.360
It was designed to be a dry, utilitarian tool for users to report system bugs to the mainframe administrators.

17:07.660 --> 17:09.340
A simple log of faults.

17:09.620 --> 17:09.800
Right.

17:09.800 --> 17:12.880
You would type, Terminal 4's screen is flickering.

17:13.480 --> 17:16.300
Or, the math drill crashed on question 7.

17:16.720 --> 17:20.900
You submit it, the admin reads it, fixes it, and that is it.

17:21.580 --> 17:25.260
But the users immediately grasped the affordance of the object.

17:25.660 --> 17:28.020
The system provided a persistent shared file.

17:28.200 --> 17:28.720
It was public.

17:29.320 --> 17:34.680
And crucially, it did not enforce any technical restrictions on what kind of text could be entered into that file.

17:34.680 --> 17:38.460
It didn't have an AI checking to make sure your text was actually a bug report.

17:39.100 --> 17:40.860
It just saved whatever text you put in.

17:41.240 --> 17:43.260
So participants started using it for other things.

17:43.500 --> 17:46.180
They started discussing strategies for the game Empire.

17:46.620 --> 17:48.820
They started discussing campus life.

17:49.260 --> 17:50.380
They debated philosophy.

17:50.720 --> 17:52.460
The interactions just accumulated.

17:53.120 --> 17:56.180
People began recognizing each other's handles and writing styles.

17:56.380 --> 17:57.880
They formed actual relationships.

17:58.360 --> 18:04.360
Plato Notes organically transformed from a bug reporting tool into a vibrant, general-purpose forum.

18:04.920 --> 18:08.040
Eventually organizing itself into discrete threads and topics.

18:08.040 --> 18:10.720
It was the direct ancestor of Reddit.

18:11.340 --> 18:12.780
Of bulletin board systems.

18:13.060 --> 18:14.160
Of modern forums.

18:14.840 --> 18:15.400
Exactly.

18:16.200 --> 18:17.740
But, hold on.

18:17.800 --> 18:19.460
Let me play devil's advocate here for a second.

18:20.280 --> 18:26.380
You have people essentially hijacking a multi-million dollar institutional machine just to hang out and play space games.

18:27.000 --> 18:28.180
That is what was happening.

18:28.780 --> 18:30.020
This wasn't a public utility.

18:30.600 --> 18:31.840
It was owned by a university.

18:32.060 --> 18:33.880
It was funded heavily by institutions.

18:34.460 --> 18:37.180
Including the military and the National Science Foundation.

18:37.180 --> 18:41.580
They didn't pour millions into mainframe development so college kids could chat all night.

18:42.260 --> 18:45.500
And that tertian is exactly what Flyxion highlights next.

18:45.800 --> 18:51.360
When educational computing resources are appropriated and become spaces for independent social organization,

18:51.360 --> 18:55.640
a profound conflict arises regarding institutional governance.

18:55.980 --> 18:56.720
Especially then.

18:57.260 --> 19:04.700
The Plato system existed during the 1960s and 70s, which were periods of intense, often volatile political activity on university campuses.

19:04.700 --> 19:09.360
Yeah, so you had the anti-war movement, the civil rights movement, massive cultural upheaval.

19:09.980 --> 19:17.300
And according to historical records cited in the paper, users were utilizing Plato's systems to organize protests against the Vietnam War.

19:17.300 --> 19:21.380
And later to host deep, ongoing discussions about the Watergate scandal.

19:21.600 --> 19:25.280
Which put the university in an incredibly difficult position.

19:25.780 --> 19:33.900
Historical accounts show that the institutional funders were not pleased that their expensive computing resources were being used to facilitate political dissent.

19:33.900 --> 19:35.480
So what did the administration do?

19:36.220 --> 19:37.900
They responded with strict crackdowns.

19:38.780 --> 19:42.700
They threatened to pull funding specifically to shut down the Watergate discussion forums.

19:43.160 --> 19:43.260
Wow.

19:43.600 --> 19:52.280
Furthermore, to combat the million plus hours being spent on games like Empire, administrators deployed a script literally named The Enforcer.

19:52.600 --> 19:54.660
The Enforcer. That sounds ominous.

19:54.660 --> 20:00.060
It actively monitored the system and terminated game processes during peak educational hours.

20:00.820 --> 20:05.440
It is the ultimate my house, my rules scenario, played out on the very first digital frontier.

20:06.380 --> 20:08.340
The institution controls the physical servers.

20:08.980 --> 20:09.800
They control the funding.

20:10.360 --> 20:15.580
So the moment the community's behavior deviates from the institution's goals, they just pull the plug on those specific objects.

20:16.000 --> 20:22.580
It demonstrates that the architecture provides the affordance, but the institution holds the ultimate power of eviction.

20:22.580 --> 20:34.080
The Plato case definitively proves that the functions we associate with social media forums, real-time chat, multiplayer, co-presence, are not inherent to communication networks.

20:34.560 --> 20:34.620
No.

20:34.940 --> 20:40.040
They were generated by a shared educational environment long before the public web even existed.

20:40.280 --> 20:40.540
Right.

20:41.240 --> 20:41.560
Okay.

20:41.700 --> 20:46.200
So the Plato story proves this happens in early isolated mainframes.

20:46.200 --> 20:53.000
But what happens when you expose this object-centered behavior to the modern, hyper-competitive internet?

20:53.680 --> 20:56.340
To see that, we have to jump forward in time to 2010.

20:56.940 --> 21:04.200
We are looking at a platform called Memrise because this case study shows exactly how this dynamic plays out on a microscopic level today.

21:04.480 --> 21:04.780
Right.

21:05.200 --> 21:07.180
Memrise launched as a language learning app.

21:07.400 --> 21:09.900
Its core mechanic was based on spaced repetition.

21:10.580 --> 21:13.520
Spaced repetition is an algorithmic scheduling technique.

21:13.520 --> 21:19.300
It tests you on vocabulary just as you are about to forget it, moving the knowledge into long-term memory.

21:19.900 --> 21:22.040
But it wasn't just a top-down curriculum, right?

21:22.120 --> 21:27.680
It grew into a platform where users could build comprehensive community courses on literally anything.

21:28.000 --> 21:35.200
You had courses on advanced mathematics, global geography, programming languages, obscure scientific terminology.

21:35.600 --> 21:38.680
It became a massive, generalized learning infrastructure.

21:38.680 --> 21:47.140
And the atomic unit of this entire infrastructure, the shared boundary object at the center of the universe, was the humble digital flashcard.

21:47.680 --> 21:50.740
The flashcard is an ideal boundary object.

21:51.400 --> 21:52.440
It is strictly bounded.

21:52.880 --> 21:53.760
It is reusable.

21:54.240 --> 21:56.520
It has a clear prompt and a correct answer.

21:57.280 --> 22:03.240
But Memrise included a very specific feature on their flashcards called a MEM, M-E-M.

22:03.340 --> 22:04.760
Short for mnemonic device.

22:05.200 --> 22:06.580
And visually, what was this?

22:06.580 --> 22:10.840
It was just a blank annotation text box attached to the bottom of the flashcard.

22:11.800 --> 22:16.860
The intended pedagogical function of the MEM was to allow users to create and share memory aids.

22:17.480 --> 22:25.460
You might upload a funny image or write a clever pun or share a short story that associates the foreign vocabulary word with a concept you already know.

22:25.880 --> 22:35.200
But, just like David R. Woolley's bug reports on Play-Doh, the MEM was a public, free text field attached to a highly trafficked object.

22:35.200 --> 22:37.740
Right. It didn't force you to write a mnemonic.

22:38.180 --> 22:40.700
It could bear any content its authors chose.

22:41.020 --> 22:45.100
Which led to a phenomenon Flaclysia describes using a biological concept.

22:46.260 --> 22:47.940
Stigmurgic coordination.

22:48.760 --> 22:49.280
Stigmurgy.

22:49.640 --> 22:49.960
Yes.

22:50.300 --> 22:53.320
I know this term originates from entomology, right?

22:53.380 --> 22:54.020
The study of insects?

22:54.020 --> 23:02.100
Yes. The term was coined by the French biologist Pierre-Paul Grasset in 1959 to describe the building behavior of termites.

23:02.340 --> 23:07.600
Let's ride this metaphor because it perfectly explains the mechanics of what happened on Memrise.

23:08.180 --> 23:13.360
How did termites build those massive, complex mounds without a foreman shouting orders?

23:13.360 --> 23:15.760
They do it without any direct communication.

23:16.280 --> 23:19.380
A termite doesn't walk up to another termite and say, hey, put a wall here.

23:19.620 --> 23:19.740
Right.

23:20.080 --> 23:24.760
Instead, they coordinate entirely by modifying their shared physical environment.

23:25.240 --> 23:30.460
A single termite will deposit a tiny pellet of mud that is infused with a specific pheromone.

23:30.740 --> 23:32.400
Okay, so the environment is altered.

23:32.760 --> 23:33.140
Exactly.

23:33.680 --> 23:37.880
That modified environment, the pheromone-laced mud pellet, acts as a stimulus.

23:37.880 --> 23:47.800
When the next termite wanders by and senses that specific pheromone gradient, it triggers an instinct to place their mud pellet on top of the first one, adding their own pheromones.

23:48.020 --> 23:50.400
So the coordination happens through the object.

23:50.820 --> 23:53.240
The environment itself is the instruction manual.

23:53.580 --> 23:54.140
Precisely.

23:54.460 --> 23:57.300
And this maps perfectly to the digital flashcard.

23:57.840 --> 24:05.840
When a user on Memrise studies a difficult Russian verb and they leave a helpful joke in the mem field, they are the termite leaving the pheromone pellet.

24:06.040 --> 24:06.680
I love this.

24:06.680 --> 24:08.560
The next learner comes along.

24:09.260 --> 24:11.000
They are struggling with the exact same verb.

24:11.740 --> 24:12.500
They see the joke.

24:12.980 --> 24:13.680
It helps them.

24:14.300 --> 24:15.640
So they click the upvote button.

24:16.300 --> 24:19.680
And that upvote makes the mem more visually prominent for the next learner.

24:20.340 --> 24:23.120
Maybe they leave a reply explaining the etymology of the verb.

24:23.840 --> 24:32.380
You are coordinating your learning, building shared knowledge, and creating a distinct social space without ever sending a direct message to another user.

24:32.380 --> 24:37.140
You are communicating entirely through the structural modification of the shared object.

24:37.140 --> 24:40.780
And this creates a vastly different user experience than a feed.

24:41.520 --> 24:48.180
On Twitter or TikTok, a ranking algorithm pushes a piece of content at you based on engagement metrics.

24:48.600 --> 24:49.400
You look at it.

24:49.840 --> 24:51.100
You scroll past it.

24:51.100 --> 24:54.000
And it is essentially gone forever into the abyss.

24:54.840 --> 24:56.060
You will likely never see it again.

24:56.060 --> 25:04.000
But on Memrise, because of the spaced repetition algorithm, you return to the exact same flashcard repeatedly over weeks and months.

25:04.000 --> 25:06.080
Here's where it gets really interesting.

25:06.460 --> 25:12.980
It is like leaving notes in the margins of a library book, but it is a library book that thousands of people are reading simultaneously.

25:13.300 --> 25:13.440
Yes.

25:13.980 --> 25:22.020
You are entering a room where generations of learners have struggled with the exact same concept, and they have left graffiti on the walls to help you out.

25:22.580 --> 25:25.800
The community builds up through sheer recurrence and shared struggle.

25:25.800 --> 25:34.280
However, Memrise also implemented a feature that perfectly illustrates a critical, often fatal design flaw in many modern platforms.

25:34.780 --> 25:39.540
They forcefully coupled the social graph with the comparison graph.

25:39.780 --> 25:41.700
Okay, let's untangle those two terms.

25:41.900 --> 25:42.860
What is a social graph?

25:43.300 --> 25:52.400
A social graph represents who you are genuinely interested in, who you want to follow, whose progress you want to monitor, whose mnemonic jokes you enjoy.

25:52.680 --> 25:54.840
It is a map of your social attention.

25:55.800 --> 25:56.980
And the comparison graph.

25:57.380 --> 26:01.760
The comparison graph determines who you are competing against on a leaderboard.

26:02.040 --> 26:03.820
It maps your relative standing.

26:04.720 --> 26:07.720
Now, on Memrise, these were not two separate systems.

26:08.420 --> 26:08.780
Uh-oh.

26:09.100 --> 26:14.460
To compete with someone on the weekly points leaderboard, you had to click Follow on their profile.

26:14.700 --> 26:19.300
Okay, so to see how I rank against the top German learners in the world, I follow them.

26:19.500 --> 26:21.640
That makes sense on the surface, but what was the catch?

26:21.640 --> 26:26.380
The catch was that Memrise hard-capped the number of people you could follow at 500.

26:26.640 --> 26:27.040
Oh, wow.

26:27.500 --> 26:37.380
So if I hit my 500 limit, and a new prodigy shows up on the platform, and I want to see how I stack up against them, wait, I have to unfollow someone else to make room.

26:37.380 --> 26:42.500
Yes, and this completely contaminates the social meaning of the follow mechanism.

26:43.280 --> 26:48.620
Unfollowing someone is no longer just a social gesture of disinterest or a way to clean up your feed.

26:48.840 --> 26:53.660
It becomes a strategic, gamified move to revise your competitive reference class.

26:54.140 --> 26:54.360
Right.

26:54.360 --> 26:55.660
Let me make sure I've got this.

26:55.660 --> 27:06.280
If I follow a really prolific learner who studies 10 hours a day, their massive point total raises the average standard against which my own progress is ranked on my personal leaderboard.

27:06.560 --> 27:07.000
Exactly.

27:07.700 --> 27:12.920
You are effectively penalizing your own competitive standing by broadening your social attention.

27:13.540 --> 27:19.080
It forces the user to choose between having friends and having an accurate, motivating leaderboard.

27:19.420 --> 27:21.900
You have to curate your friends list as a weapon.

27:21.900 --> 27:29.560
It is a sociological governance decision built right into the code, and it fundamentally alters human behavior on the platform.

27:30.200 --> 27:35.500
Which brings us to the most precarious reality of relying on centralized digital spaces.

27:36.040 --> 27:41.680
What happens when the company that owns the server decides they don't like the graffiti in the library books anymore?

27:42.160 --> 27:45.880
This is where the story of Memrise takes a dark turn for its community.

27:45.880 --> 27:52.480
Between 2021 and 2024, the company ruled out a series of massive structural changes.

27:52.800 --> 27:52.900
Right.

27:53.300 --> 27:55.760
They systematically removed the community MEMS.

27:56.220 --> 27:59.300
They shut down the community forum where people discussed the courses.

27:59.820 --> 28:08.080
And eventually, they moved all the user-created community courses off the main app entirely, separating them into a hard-to-find web-only interface.

28:08.080 --> 28:12.160
The paper formally terms this event affordance foreclosure.

28:13.120 --> 28:20.200
It is the unilateral elimination of a platform capability that supports user activity beyond the operator's preferred official use.

28:20.840 --> 28:24.380
Now, to be objective, Memrise wasn't doing this just to be evil.

28:24.860 --> 28:27.820
They had standard, rational business reasons for this pivot.

28:28.040 --> 28:28.360
Absolutely.

28:29.120 --> 28:32.520
The paper outlines several common rationales for affordance foreclosure.

28:32.860 --> 28:34.620
Product specialization is one.

28:34.620 --> 28:42.200
The company may have wanted to pivot strictly to selling premium, officially curated language courses rather than hosting amateur content.

28:42.720 --> 28:45.220
Moderation costs are a massive factor, too.

28:45.540 --> 28:53.380
Anytime you allow user-generated free text, you have to actively police it for spam, abuse, and illegal content, which requires expensive human labor.

28:53.740 --> 28:55.120
And finally, commercial positioning.

28:55.120 --> 29:04.280
It is often much easier to sell a subscription to a sleek, controlled, predictable product than to a chaotic, user-generated bazaar.

29:04.620 --> 29:06.580
We aren't debating the business logic here.

29:06.980 --> 29:09.760
But the social impact was devastating.

29:10.180 --> 29:11.940
It annihilated the shared space.

29:11.940 --> 29:20.400
The deep annotations, the inside jokes, the etymological debates were users that spent years building localized reputations.

29:20.800 --> 29:23.420
All of it was suddenly rendered inaccessible.

29:23.420 --> 29:34.160
To understand why this feels so profoundly unjust to the users, Flyxion breaks down the governance of shared computational objects into a highly useful four-part tuple.

29:34.780 --> 29:37.760
Auth, rev, appeal, and exit.

29:37.980 --> 29:38.180
Right.

29:38.700 --> 29:38.940
Okay.

29:39.180 --> 29:42.720
Break out the ELF Y5 for this framework because it is brilliant.

29:43.160 --> 29:44.140
Let's start with auth.

29:44.820 --> 29:47.000
Auth stands for authorial authority.

29:47.000 --> 29:50.260
It answers the question, who is permitted to contribute?

29:50.680 --> 29:54.480
Who is allowed to write a mem or build a flashcard or upload an image?

29:55.280 --> 29:57.980
In the golden age of Memrise, the users head off.

29:58.540 --> 29:59.440
Anyone could contribute.

29:59.780 --> 30:00.060
Correct.

30:00.760 --> 30:04.180
The second component is REV, which stands for revisionary authority.

30:04.940 --> 30:10.440
This is meta authority who has the power to change the fundamental rules of the system itself,

30:10.640 --> 30:14.780
who can alter the algorithm or remove a feature or delete a database.

30:14.780 --> 30:17.500
That is exclusively the platform operator.

30:17.800 --> 30:19.780
The company holds 100% of the REV tut.

30:20.440 --> 30:21.340
The third is appeal.

30:22.060 --> 30:28.320
If the operator uses their REV tut to make a change you hate, what is the formal mechanism for contesting that decision?

30:28.920 --> 30:32.540
For almost all social media, appeal basically means shouting into the void on Twitter

30:32.540 --> 30:36.760
or starting a change.org petition that the company will completely ignore.

30:37.220 --> 30:40.760
There is no binding legal mechanism for users to overrule the operator.

30:41.060 --> 30:41.420
Exactly.

30:41.840 --> 30:43.660
And the final component is exit.

30:43.660 --> 30:50.540
When you finally decide to leave or when you are forced out, what are you technically and legally allowed to take with you?

30:50.860 --> 30:55.520
Can you export your data, your social graph, your reputation in a format that works elsewhere?

30:56.420 --> 30:57.700
So what does this all mean?

30:58.280 --> 31:02.840
What this tuple reveals is the massive chasm between three distinct concepts.

31:03.420 --> 31:06.360
Authorship, stewardship, and ownership.

31:06.740 --> 31:07.240
Precisely.

31:07.240 --> 31:09.600
The users provided the authorship.

31:09.880 --> 31:10.620
They wrote the content.

31:10.920 --> 31:12.720
The community provided the stewardship.

31:13.060 --> 31:16.400
They upvoted, moderated, and maintained the cultural norms of the space.

31:16.880 --> 31:19.200
But the company retained absolute ownership.

31:19.440 --> 31:23.720
The unilateral, unchallengeable power to delete the entire ecosystem overnight.

31:23.720 --> 31:29.620
So, if I spend five years of my life meticulously building a specialized course on organic chemistry,

31:30.100 --> 31:34.700
and I build a massive reputation helping thousands of students in the MEMS,

31:34.820 --> 31:37.500
I am ultimately just a digital tenant.

31:38.180 --> 31:39.480
I don't own my house.

31:40.480 --> 31:44.620
I am waiting to be evicted because the landlord decided they wanted to bulldoze my apartment building

31:44.620 --> 31:46.480
and put up a premium parking lot.

31:46.480 --> 31:49.300
That is the harsh reality of centralized platforms.

31:49.860 --> 31:54.180
The users have localized auth, but the operator holds absolute rev.

31:54.820 --> 32:00.280
There is virtually no appeal, and the exit is usually limited to downloading a useless,

32:00.640 --> 32:02.660
unformatted CSV file of your data.

32:03.020 --> 32:04.900
But it doesn't have to be this way.

32:05.120 --> 32:07.540
And this is where the paper draws an incredible contrast.

32:08.340 --> 32:09.920
Let's go back to Plato for a second.

32:10.500 --> 32:13.240
The commercial life of the Plato system eventually wound down.

32:13.440 --> 32:15.920
Controlled Data Corporation stopped supporting it.

32:15.920 --> 32:18.400
The physical mainframes were decommissioned.

32:18.480 --> 32:19.920
The landlord tore down the building.

32:20.120 --> 32:20.320
Right.

32:21.140 --> 32:22.400
But the community survived.

32:23.240 --> 32:27.780
In 2004, a group of dedicated former users resurrected the Plato system online

32:27.780 --> 32:31.840
using an open-source emulator, a project they called CyberOne.

32:32.060 --> 32:32.660
And what happened?

32:33.200 --> 32:37.080
Within six months, hundreds of former users who hadn't spoken in years

32:37.080 --> 32:41.400
found the emulator, signed up, and seamlessly recreated their community.

32:41.820 --> 32:43.940
They picked up right where they left off.

32:43.940 --> 32:47.700
Why did the Plato community survive the destruction of their original hardware

32:47.700 --> 32:52.500
while the Memrise community courses fractured and died when features were changed?

32:52.700 --> 32:52.760
Why?

32:53.240 --> 32:58.660
Because the CyberOne revival preserved the mechanism, the objects, and the access together.

32:59.140 --> 33:02.140
It was an exact replication of the shared environment.

33:02.140 --> 33:06.200
In contrast, when Memrise faced immense user backlash

33:06.200 --> 33:11.540
and eventually brought back a heavily revised version of the Memrise feature in 2026,

33:11.740 --> 33:13.780
the original community did not return.

33:14.320 --> 33:14.980
They just moved on.

33:15.500 --> 33:19.060
You cannot just restore a graphical button on a user interface

33:19.060 --> 33:23.560
and expect a dispersed, disillusioned community to instantly materialize.

33:23.560 --> 33:26.560
A software feature is just a technical mechanism,

33:26.780 --> 33:32.480
but a social function is a living relationship between that mechanism and a trusting community.

33:33.120 --> 33:37.920
Once the trust is broken and the shared history is severed, the sociality dies.

33:38.200 --> 33:40.520
Which naturally leads to the biggest question.

33:41.100 --> 33:44.960
If centralized platforms are essentially digital dictatorships,

33:45.160 --> 33:48.380
where we can be evicted at any time, what are the alternatives?

33:48.380 --> 33:53.480
How do the most resilient, enduring platforms on the Internet actually function under the hood?

33:54.180 --> 33:59.840
To answer that, Flyxion conducts a comparative architectural analysis of three massive systems.

34:00.440 --> 34:04.100
Wikipedia, GitHub, and Multiplayer Digital Worlds.

34:04.480 --> 34:04.800
Okay.

34:05.120 --> 34:10.820
These systems prove beyond a doubt that object-centered sociality is not only possible at scale,

34:10.980 --> 34:13.700
it is vastly more robust than the feed model.

34:13.980 --> 34:15.020
Let's start with Wikipedia.

34:15.020 --> 34:19.180
It is the purest, most successful, large-scale example of this theory in existence.

34:19.940 --> 34:23.060
On Wikipedia, the shared object is the encyclopedic article.

34:23.680 --> 34:26.060
There is no feed of your friend's recent edits.

34:26.660 --> 34:31.260
And crucially, Wikipedia proves that you do not need rich, prominent personal profiles

34:31.260 --> 34:34.300
to organize a massive, complex social project.

34:34.880 --> 34:39.060
Many of the most prolific and respected editors on Wikipedia operate under pseudonyms.

34:39.600 --> 34:40.800
Many don't even have accounts.

34:41.020 --> 34:43.480
They edit solely under anonymous IP addresses.

34:43.480 --> 34:49.960
If Wikipedia is the ultimate example of healthy sociality, why is it notoriously hostile to new users?

34:50.760 --> 34:56.800
Doesn't the talk page just become a different kind of toxic feed for power users who want to guard their territory?

34:57.240 --> 34:58.600
That is a very fair critique.

34:59.040 --> 35:00.460
Wikipedia is not a utopia.

35:00.980 --> 35:03.020
It has intense internal politics.

35:03.340 --> 35:05.620
But the nature of the conflict is different.

35:06.260 --> 35:08.440
On a Twitter feed, conflict is performative.

35:08.760 --> 35:11.360
It is designed to generate engagement from an audience.

35:11.680 --> 35:11.860
Right.

35:11.860 --> 35:15.540
On a Wikipedia talk page, the conflict is teleological.

35:16.240 --> 35:18.740
It is aimed at resolving the state of the shared object.

35:19.400 --> 35:22.340
The editors have standing, not because they have a million followers,

35:22.740 --> 35:27.740
but because their history of localized edits is a durable, verifiable record of stewardship.

35:28.380 --> 35:31.880
The pristine, public article you read is merely the visible surface.

35:32.340 --> 35:38.320
Beneath it lies a deep, turbulent history of conflict, cooperation, vandalism, and reversion.

35:38.320 --> 35:39.880
Then there is GitHub.

35:41.160 --> 35:46.200
For anyone not in software engineering, GitHub is basically social media for developers.

35:46.660 --> 35:54.780
But instead of posting photos of your lunch to get likes, your feed is the actual blood, sweat, and tears of the technical work you are doing together.

35:54.780 --> 36:00.800
On GitHub, the repositories, the folders holding the code, are the shared boundary objects.

36:01.400 --> 36:06.260
The social mechanisms are things like pull requests, commits, and issue trackers.

36:06.460 --> 36:07.620
Break down a pull request.

36:08.060 --> 36:09.920
How is submitting code a social act?

36:09.920 --> 36:15.660
When a developer writes a new feature and submits a pull request, they are proposing a change to the shared object.

36:16.000 --> 36:18.440
But it is not just a blind file transfer.

36:18.800 --> 36:23.220
It is a highly formalized communicative act addressed to the project maintainers.

36:23.360 --> 36:23.480
Right.

36:23.700 --> 36:31.440
It says, I have observed a need, I have done this work, and I request that you integrate my reality into the shared reality.

36:31.440 --> 36:39.540
The platform organizes reputation, mentorship, and debate entirely around the shared work, rather than around personal broadcasting.

36:40.100 --> 36:41.080
And what about YouTube?

36:41.860 --> 36:43.620
Where does video fit into this framework?

36:44.460 --> 36:46.240
YouTube is a fascinating hybrid.

36:46.660 --> 36:51.080
The video itself functions as a publication, a static object broadcast to many.

36:51.580 --> 36:57.440
But the comment thread beneath the video is a form of annotation, structurally similar to the memes on Memrise.

36:57.860 --> 37:01.140
So the comments are socially attached to the primary object.

37:01.440 --> 37:06.560
Furthermore, reaction videos or musical remixes are a form of collaborative production.

37:07.360 --> 37:10.860
YouTube certainly employs aggressive, feed-like recommendation algorithms.

37:11.220 --> 37:15.960
But the core social interaction is still deeply anchored to persistent objects.

37:16.440 --> 37:17.980
And then my personal favorite category.

37:18.800 --> 37:19.680
Multiplayer games.

37:20.120 --> 37:22.760
Minecraft, EVE Online, Roblox.

37:23.080 --> 37:25.280
This is where Phloxion's theory gets visceral.

37:25.600 --> 37:30.500
This is where we observe sociality through co-presence and consequential action.

37:30.500 --> 37:40.380
If you and I are logged into the same persistent Minecraft server, and I spend five hours building a massive stone wall across a valley, I have fundamentally altered your reality.

37:40.920 --> 37:47.040
The next time I log in, I literally have to walk around your wall, or spend 20 minutes mining a tunnel through it.

37:47.780 --> 37:49.140
I never sent you a chat message.

37:49.420 --> 37:50.740
I didn't tag you in a post.

37:51.240 --> 37:53.060
We might not even speak the same language.

37:53.060 --> 37:58.800
But my physical action in the digital space was deeply, causally consequential to your experience.

37:59.240 --> 37:59.640
Exactly.

38:00.100 --> 38:01.820
The action is the communication.

38:02.400 --> 38:06.340
Phloxion categorizes these diverse mechanisms into four distinct buckets.

38:07.000 --> 38:08.740
Publication, like a YouTube video.

38:09.460 --> 38:11.800
Annotation, like a comment or a mem.

38:12.760 --> 38:15.020
Collaboration, like a GitHub pull request.

38:15.480 --> 38:18.160
And co-presence, like building a wall in Minecraft.

38:18.160 --> 38:23.100
And none of those four mechanisms resemble scrolling a ranked feed of hot takes.

38:23.320 --> 38:23.660
No.

38:24.500 --> 38:31.940
And underlying all of these robust platforms is a critical computer science concept that Phloxion highlights called event sourcing.

38:32.480 --> 38:35.060
Yes, event sourcing and the resulting history dependence.

38:35.760 --> 38:36.800
This is vital to understand.

38:36.800 --> 38:43.740
In a traditional database, if you change your address, the system deletes your old address and overwrites it with the new one.

38:44.140 --> 38:45.800
The past is just erased.

38:45.800 --> 38:49.040
But in an event sourced architecture, it works differently.

38:49.580 --> 38:51.180
Think of it like a bank account ledger.

38:51.580 --> 38:54.200
Your bank doesn't just store a single number representing your balance.

38:54.420 --> 38:58.540
They store an immutable list of every single transaction you have ever made.

38:59.200 --> 39:06.460
Your current balance of $500 is just a temporary projection, calculated by adding and subtracting that entire history of events.

39:06.760 --> 39:07.220
Precisely.

39:07.640 --> 39:11.600
GitHub operates exactly like this, through the Git version control system.

39:12.300 --> 39:14.780
Wikipedia operates like this through its revision history.

39:14.780 --> 39:19.720
The true state of the system is the entire sequence of events that have ever happened.

39:20.440 --> 39:23.920
The history isn't just a backup file kept in case of a server crash.

39:24.340 --> 39:27.460
The history is the actual substance of the community.

39:27.980 --> 39:29.920
It is the proof of their shared labor.

39:30.560 --> 39:37.220
Which is why losing that history, or even losing the symbolic identity attached to that history, is so devastating to a community.

39:37.220 --> 39:42.620
The paper briefly touches on Twitter's rebranding to X in 2023 as an example of this.

39:43.280 --> 39:43.400
Right.

39:43.980 --> 39:49.560
Elon Musk buys the platform, kills the iconic blue bird, and changes the name to a single letter.

39:50.240 --> 39:53.040
Flyxion cites an earlier 2023 essay titled,

39:53.040 --> 40:00.440
What the Birds are Tweeting, which argues that over 15 years, Twitter had built a highly coherent metaphorical vocabulary.

40:01.180 --> 40:04.620
You had the bird logo, you sent tweets, you read your timeline.

40:04.980 --> 40:07.000
If a post was popular, it took flight.

40:07.420 --> 40:09.240
It was a whole conceptual universe.

40:09.780 --> 40:12.620
That vocabulary wasn't just a corporate branding label.

40:12.620 --> 40:16.540
It was deeply intertwined with how the platform shaped its social practices.

40:17.420 --> 40:23.780
The sudden unilateral erasure of that conceptual metaphor by the owner is a form of affordance foreclosure.

40:24.180 --> 40:30.300
It is the loss of a shared symbolic identity, which instantly degrades the community's sense of belonging to that space.

40:30.620 --> 40:30.940
Exactly.

40:31.520 --> 40:36.720
Okay, so we have firmly established how human-made shared objects build community.

40:37.080 --> 40:41.860
We see it in the code of GitHub, the talk pages of wikis, the walls of Minecraft.

40:41.860 --> 40:49.240
But we have to address the massive generative elephant in the room, artificial intelligence.

40:49.640 --> 40:49.720
Right.

40:50.240 --> 40:58.140
What happens to our shared digital reality when generative AI can instantly create infinite, perfectly personalized objects?

40:58.640 --> 41:02.880
This is where the paper becomes incredibly prescient about our immediate future.

41:03.920 --> 41:07.040
Flyxion examines systems like Google's Notebook LM,

41:07.040 --> 41:19.480
which can take a dense textbook or a stack of unstructured research papers and instantly transform them into a massive deck of learning cards or a custom quiz or even a generated audio discussion.

41:19.480 --> 41:23.980
On the surface, if I am a student or teacher, that sounds like a miracle.

41:24.380 --> 41:28.820
It completely eliminates the grueling labor of creating those shared boundary objects.

41:28.940 --> 41:34.280
We don't need a hundred volunteers to spend a month meticulously building a memorized course anymore.

41:34.840 --> 41:37.800
The AI generates it flawlessly in five seconds.

41:37.800 --> 41:38.660
It does.

41:39.340 --> 41:41.900
And the utilitarian value of that is undeniable.

41:42.580 --> 41:47.020
But it introduces a profound structural threat to sociality.

41:47.520 --> 41:50.740
The phenomenon of personalized generation.

41:51.160 --> 41:52.200
Personalized generation.

41:52.860 --> 42:00.720
If a centralized AI analyzes my profile and generates a completely custom flashcard deck tailored precisely to my learning style,

42:00.720 --> 42:05.540
and then it generates a completely different, uniquely tailored deck for you,

42:06.460 --> 42:09.420
the shared boundary object ceases to exist.

42:09.980 --> 42:11.380
But wait, let me push back hard on this.

42:11.780 --> 42:16.800
Isn't hyper-personalization the absolute holy grail of educational technology?

42:17.320 --> 42:19.700
Every teacher wants to give individualized instruction.

42:20.420 --> 42:25.860
Why is having my own perfectly tailored textbook suddenly a dystopian threat to society?

42:26.140 --> 42:29.880
Because if we do not have a shared object, we cannot have a shared reality.

42:29.880 --> 42:36.400
To explain this mechanically, Flyxion breaks down human interaction with media into three layers of shared reference.

42:37.180 --> 42:38.440
Walk us through the three layers.

42:39.100 --> 42:43.860
Layer one is the authoritative object, the actual source text, or the physical specimen.

42:44.740 --> 42:49.640
Layer two is the presentation, how that object is formatted or displayed to the user.

42:50.460 --> 42:54.780
Layer three is the interpretation, what the user thinks the object means.

42:55.440 --> 42:57.280
Okay, let's use a concrete example.

42:57.560 --> 42:59.100
We are both studying Shakespeare.

42:59.100 --> 43:03.160
We both buy the exact same Penguin Classics edition of Hamlet.

43:03.680 --> 43:07.620
That physical text is layer one, the authoritative object.

43:07.980 --> 43:08.200
Correct.

43:08.900 --> 43:12.800
Now, imagine you are reading the paperback version, but I am legally blind,

43:12.940 --> 43:15.680
so I am listening to an unabridged audiobook version.

43:16.060 --> 43:18.440
We are experiencing different presentations.

43:19.120 --> 43:19.960
That is layer two.

43:20.480 --> 43:22.460
And layer three is interpretation.

43:22.460 --> 43:28.660
We get into a heated argument because I think Hamlet is feigning madness as a tactical strategy.

43:29.040 --> 43:32.140
And you think he is genuinely suffering a psychotic break.

43:32.560 --> 43:33.000
Exactly.

43:33.640 --> 43:38.460
Disagreeing on interpretation at layer three is the normal, healthy condition of human discourse.

43:39.000 --> 43:40.100
It is why we have discussions.

43:40.640 --> 43:41.300
We argue.

43:41.300 --> 43:42.820
We cite evidence from the text.

43:43.040 --> 43:44.560
We try to persuade each other.

43:45.020 --> 43:47.220
But what does generative AI do to this stack?

43:48.260 --> 43:53.340
Personalized generation violently attacks layer one, but it tries to hide the damage.

43:53.860 --> 43:56.420
Imagine an AI tutor is assigned to help us study.

43:57.460 --> 44:01.000
The AI knows that student A struggles with complex 16th century vocabulary,

44:01.000 --> 44:04.720
so it silently rewrites Hamlet into the modern vernacular.

44:05.580 --> 44:07.940
The AI knows student B has a short attention span,

44:08.060 --> 44:11.160
so it silently summarizes entire acts into bullet points.

44:11.660 --> 44:13.960
But the system interface still just calls it Hamlet.

44:14.080 --> 44:14.380
Yes.

44:14.980 --> 44:19.320
If the system silently rewrites the text to pander to our individual profiles,

44:19.840 --> 44:23.500
but presents it as if we are looking at the same authoritative object,

44:23.680 --> 44:27.140
we lose the fundamental ability to have a productive disagreement.

44:27.340 --> 44:27.680
Oh, wow.

44:27.680 --> 44:32.700
If we try to debate Hamlet's motivations, we will literally be talking past each other.

44:33.240 --> 44:35.440
The bridge between our minds, the shared object,

44:35.560 --> 44:39.840
has been quietly burned by the algorithm to optimize for our individual comfort.

44:40.340 --> 44:43.780
We are arguing about entirely different things, but we don't realize it.

44:44.040 --> 44:47.160
We are trapped in our own private generated bubbles.

44:47.420 --> 44:49.420
That is terrifying.

44:49.780 --> 44:54.260
It means if we apply this to history or civics or news,

44:54.260 --> 44:58.180
you could have two people violently arguing over a political concept,

44:58.740 --> 45:02.640
completely unaware that the AI dynamically rewrote their source materials

45:02.640 --> 45:05.300
to cater to their pre-existing political biases.

45:06.000 --> 45:10.180
It erases the common ground required for communication to even occur.

45:10.540 --> 45:17.140
This is why the paper forcefully argues that a learning network can only act as a distributed epistemic institution,

45:17.140 --> 45:22.880
which is a fancy way of saying a community that collectively evaluates what is true and what is false,

45:22.880 --> 45:26.620
if the AI is forced to maintain strict interpretive restraint.

45:27.100 --> 45:28.300
Interpretive restraint.

45:29.060 --> 45:32.480
How does a developer actually build that into code?

45:32.580 --> 45:33.580
What does it mean mechanically?

45:33.580 --> 45:39.400
It means the system architecture must rigidly preserve the difference between the observed signal,

45:40.080 --> 45:43.400
the generated representation, and the attributed meaning.

45:44.020 --> 45:46.900
If an AI hallucinates a connection between two concepts,

45:47.300 --> 45:52.200
the user interface must loudly and explicitly label it as a generated interpretation,

45:52.640 --> 45:55.720
not as a factual extraction derived from the source text.

45:55.960 --> 46:00.540
The system must maintain a transparent, verifiable record of provenance.

46:00.540 --> 46:05.020
It must say, I generated this specific analogy because of prompt X.

46:05.440 --> 46:05.820
Exactly.

46:06.680 --> 46:08.620
If the system hides its generative hand,

46:09.100 --> 46:12.900
hyper-personalization destroys the intersubjectivity of the community.

46:13.380 --> 46:16.920
Okay, so we have completely decoupled social media from the feed.

46:18.000 --> 46:22.820
We have decoupled it from the necessity of human authorship navigating the AI minefield.

46:23.580 --> 46:27.720
For this final section, Flyxion does something truly mind-bending.

46:27.720 --> 46:32.240
Let's decouple social media from the screen entirely.

46:32.940 --> 46:36.880
What's fascinating here is the philosophical and technical exploration

46:36.880 --> 46:39.880
of the historical contingency of visual interfaces.

46:40.560 --> 46:44.520
We all assume that to use a computer to participate in digital society,

46:44.820 --> 46:46.100
you must have a screen.

46:46.200 --> 46:47.740
You must look at a glowing rectangle.

46:48.000 --> 46:48.120
Right.

46:48.120 --> 46:52.960
But Flyxion points out that this is just a historical accident based on the limitations

46:52.960 --> 46:55.540
of early hardware choices and cathode ray tubes.

46:56.540 --> 47:02.540
The paper references a totally separate 2023 proposal written by Flyxion for an auditory

47:02.540 --> 47:06.020
operating system, an operating system that you only hear.

47:06.280 --> 47:11.480
It is based on the ancient mnemonic technique known as the memory palace or the method of loci.

47:11.480 --> 47:17.600
In this proposed purely auditory environment, data entities and system states do not have

47:17.600 --> 47:18.260
visual icons.

47:18.580 --> 47:21.440
They have characteristic acoustic identities.

47:22.300 --> 47:27.260
So instead of looking for a little yellow folder icon on a desktop, you might hear a specific

47:27.260 --> 47:30.240
sequence of bird calls to represent a directory.

47:30.520 --> 47:30.880
Exactly.

47:31.340 --> 47:35.260
The spatial relationships of the data are mapped to a 3D audio soundscape.

47:35.860 --> 47:37.640
Distance is conveyed by loudness.

47:37.640 --> 47:40.020
A file you use often sounds close.

47:40.500 --> 47:42.700
An archived file sounds faint and far away.

47:43.440 --> 47:46.760
Urgency or error states are conveyed by pitch and rhythm.

47:47.480 --> 47:51.680
And the presence of other users in the system is conveyed by environmental sounds, perhaps

47:51.680 --> 47:54.960
the rustling of leaves or the hum of a distant train.

47:55.700 --> 47:56.000
Yes.

47:56.660 --> 47:56.960
Okay.

47:57.420 --> 48:00.600
This sounds like an incredible accessibility tool for the visually impaired.

48:00.960 --> 48:03.880
But how does this apply to redefining social media?

48:03.880 --> 48:08.360
It applies through a concept Flyxion calls the SpherePOP framework.

48:09.040 --> 48:15.240
This framework forces us to separate observation, interpretation, representation, and communication.

48:15.700 --> 48:20.340
The core insight is that the social structure of a digital environment is not identical to

48:20.340 --> 48:21.520
its sensory presentation.

48:22.680 --> 48:28.080
The actual social structure is made of the data objects, the user actions, and the event-sourced

48:28.080 --> 48:28.500
histories.

48:28.500 --> 48:33.260
The visual screen is just one arbitrary way to represent that underlying structure to

48:33.260 --> 48:33.880
a human brain.

48:34.440 --> 48:36.520
Sound is another perfectly valid way.

48:37.480 --> 48:41.060
So, it is like we have been confusing the window with the actual house.

48:41.680 --> 48:45.560
The screen on my iPhone is just one specific window peering into the database.

48:46.340 --> 48:50.320
The actual social media, the community, is the house itself.

48:50.720 --> 48:54.100
And you can walk through the front door of that house using just your ears.

48:54.100 --> 48:56.200
That is the perfect analogy.

48:56.780 --> 49:00.220
Imagine navigating an auditory Wikipedia using the system.

49:00.420 --> 49:03.160
You walk into the directory for climate change.

49:03.340 --> 49:08.700
Because that specific article is a highly contested, frequently edited, heavily trafficked

49:08.700 --> 49:09.460
shared object.

49:09.780 --> 49:12.400
It has a massive amount of event history.

49:13.240 --> 49:14.500
So, acoustically, what would that be like?

49:14.960 --> 49:18.040
You might hear a bustling, chaotic crowd of signals.

49:18.520 --> 49:20.800
Heavy construction sounds representing edits.

49:20.800 --> 49:23.580
It's urgent high pitches representing reverts.

49:23.940 --> 49:26.800
A dense hum of presence representing active readers.

49:27.680 --> 49:29.860
You can physically hear the social tension and collaboration.

49:30.520 --> 49:34.820
But if you navigate away to an obscure page about a minor 14th century poet that hasn't

49:34.820 --> 49:36.160
been edited in 10 years.

49:36.540 --> 49:38.040
The soundscape changes entirely.

49:38.340 --> 49:39.560
It is just a quiet echo.

49:40.000 --> 49:40.800
A faint breeze.

49:41.620 --> 49:46.080
The depth of the history and the intensity of the social activity are communicated entirely

49:46.080 --> 49:47.520
through the auditory environment.

49:47.520 --> 49:50.760
Without you ever reading a single word of text on the screen.

49:51.660 --> 49:52.260
That is incredible.

49:52.980 --> 49:54.720
The event history is shared universally.

49:55.460 --> 49:57.220
But the presentation is entirely flexible.

49:57.940 --> 50:03.000
It means a person using a standard visual screen, a person using a text-based screen reader,

50:03.520 --> 50:08.740
and a person immersed in this purely 3D auditory interface can all inhabit the exact same social

50:08.740 --> 50:09.100
world.

50:09.300 --> 50:13.560
They can interact with the exact same shared objects and collaborate perfectly as long as

50:13.560 --> 50:16.660
that underlying event history is preserved and translated accurately.

50:16.660 --> 50:21.340
It fundamentally forces us to expand what it means to be online.

50:22.300 --> 50:24.840
The visual medium is just one option for representation.

50:25.740 --> 50:27.860
True sociality is substrate independent.

50:28.560 --> 50:31.240
It lives in the shared history, not in the pixels.

50:31.700 --> 50:32.400
Exactly right.

50:33.000 --> 50:36.460
Okay, let's tie all these incredible threads together.

50:37.220 --> 50:42.720
What Flyxion's paper shows us definitively is that social media is not a ranked feed.

50:43.360 --> 50:46.020
It is not a carefully curated personal profile.

50:46.020 --> 50:49.420
It is not a like button or a retweet or a follower count.

50:50.180 --> 50:54.000
Social media is an emergent property of shared worlds.

50:54.000 --> 50:59.020
It exists in the margins of digital flashcards where strangers help each other memorize verbs.

50:59.220 --> 51:03.760
It exists in the complex commit logs of open source software repositories on GitHub.

51:04.520 --> 51:10.440
It exists in the blocky player-built architecture of digital games, the heated talk pages of wikis,

51:10.640 --> 51:14.740
and potentially in the spatial auditory echoes of future operating systems.

51:14.740 --> 51:22.100
So, the next time you unlock your phone and open an app, I want you to ask yourself a critical question.

51:22.100 --> 51:28.360
Am I just consuming a ranked feed, or am I actually modifying a shared object?

51:29.360 --> 51:36.540
Are you just a passive audience member sitting in a digital theater, or are you an active builder in a shared world?

51:36.540 --> 51:43.240
It is the stark difference between a system mathematically designed to extract your attention for advertising revenue,

51:43.440 --> 51:48.120
and a system structurally designed to host your collaboration and preserve your labor.

51:48.520 --> 51:52.700
And that leads me to one final, deeply provocative thought I want to leave you with today.

51:53.380 --> 51:58.140
If true social connection relies on these shared objects and their deeply retained histories,

51:58.640 --> 52:02.560
what happens to our digital ghosts when the servers finally shut down?

52:02.560 --> 52:04.740
It is a profound sociological question.

52:05.180 --> 52:05.720
Think about it.

52:06.380 --> 52:11.880
A hundred years from now, will future digital archaeologists dig up our algorithms and our feeds?

52:12.740 --> 52:12.940
No.

52:13.740 --> 52:15.440
The feed changes every millisecond.

52:15.640 --> 52:17.120
It leaves no durable trace.

52:17.220 --> 52:18.880
It is ephemeral by its very design.

52:19.040 --> 52:19.280
Right.

52:19.680 --> 52:25.920
Or, will the only proof that we even existed on this network be the digital boundary objects we collectively built?

52:26.440 --> 52:27.740
The wikis we argued over.

52:27.820 --> 52:29.460
The code we carefully annotated.

52:29.840 --> 52:31.420
The Minecraft worlds we modified.

52:31.420 --> 52:34.720
The artifacts of our shared histories will be the only things that endure.

52:35.540 --> 52:37.720
The feed erases you the moment you scroll past.

52:38.220 --> 52:40.980
The shared object immortalizes you in its history.

52:41.700 --> 52:44.420
Remember the muddy waters of the medical diagnosis we started with.

52:45.000 --> 52:47.160
It is time to throw out the broken x-ray machine.

52:47.800 --> 52:49.800
Stop looking for your community in a feed.

52:50.240 --> 52:51.520
Start looking for the objects.

52:52.000 --> 52:53.200
Start looking for the architecture.

52:54.160 --> 52:56.180
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

52:56.460 --> 52:57.280
We'll see you next time.

